ey": 1.25}, "rating_margin": 20.0, "sigma_race": 6.5, "sigma_total": 7.28, "baseline_margin": 17.74, "analyst_adjustment": -1.24, "key_factors": ["Casten is a two-term incumbent with a large cash advantage ($1.3M vs $23K)", "District leans Democratic (D+3 PVI) in a favorable environment for Democrats", "Polymarket prices Casten at 96% to win", "Conforti's minimal fundraising limits her ability to compete"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point strongly to Casten: a Democratic-leaning suburban district, incumbency, a favorable national environment, and a roughly 40-to-1 fundraising edge over Conforti, who has almost no cash on hand. Prediction markets agree, pricing his win odds near 96%, so the race stays firmly in Safe D territory.", "watch": "A late surge of outside spending for Conforti or a sharp shift in the national environment toward Republicans could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Sean Casten (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Sean Casten seeks another term in Illinois' sixth district, a suburban seat that leans about three points Democratic. He won his primary easily and enters the general election with a decisive financial edge, having raised over $2 million and holding $1.3 million on hand, while Republican Niki Conforti has raised about $313,000 with little cash remaining. No outside groups have invested meaningfully in the race, and no public general-election polling is available.\n\nPrediction markets price Casten's chances at roughly 96 percent, and the favorable national environment for Democrats adds to his structural advantages. The main uncertainty is the absence of polling, but the combination of incumbency, money, and district lean makes an upset unlikely.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9821, "margin": 14.79}, {"date": "2026-09